<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">That would make the essay much more persuasive for both mathematicians and engineers. Do you want me to make those diagrams?
Alright â€” hereâ€™s a Mathematical Appendix for Entropy as Infrastructure that grounds your semantic infrastructure, CLIO integration, and thermodynamic compute-for-heat proposal in rigorous categorical and physical formalism.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Mathematical Appendix</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A. Semantic Infrastructure in a Fibered Symmetric Monoidal Category</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">We define the category of semantic modules  as a fibered symmetric monoidal category over a base category  of theoretical domains (e.g., RSVP, SIT, CoM, RAT).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.1 Objects and Morphisms</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">An object  is a quadruple:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">M = (F, \Sigma, D, \varphi)</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&nbsp;is a finite set of function hashes uniquely identifying computational operations.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&nbsp;is a set of semantic type annotations.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&nbsp;is a directed acyclic dependency graph with vertices for submodules.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&nbsp;is an entropy mapping assigning each function to an observable  in the RSVP field.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A morphism  preserves entropy and semantic typing:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\forall \mu \in M, \quad S(f(\mu)) \le S(\mu), \quad \Sigma(\mu) \subseteq \Sigma(f(\mu)).</p>
