<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Chapter 5 â€” From Wasted Cycles to Useful Heat: A Global Polycomputation Policy Framework</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">5.1 Introduction</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The 21st century has entered a paradoxical thermodynamic era:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Humanity expends unprecedented amounts of energy on cryptographic proof-of-work computations whose only purpose is self-validation of a ledger.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Simultaneously, we continue to operate millions of discrete heaters, heat pumps, and HVAC systems whose thermodynamic output is functionally identical to the waste heat from computing hardware â€” yet these devices perform no computationally useful work.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This chapter proposes a categorical and policy framework to merge thermoregulation with computation at planetary scale, ensuring that no heat is wasted without producing epistemically valuable computation. In doing so, we implement entropy as infrastructure, where every unit of thermal dissipation is also an investment in knowledge production, environmental modeling, or compression of the informational state of the universe.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">5.2 Policy Premise</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">We posit the following axiomatic policy:</p>
